<article class="env-lemma" id="0FXK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FXK">Lemma <span data-tag="0FXK">15.81.4</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Noetherian regular ring of dimension $1 \leq d < \infty $. Let $K \in D(R)$ be perfect and let $k \in \mathbf{Z}$ such that $H^ i(K) = 0$ for $i = k - d + 2, \ldots , k$ (empty condition if $d = 1$). Then $K = \tau _{\leq k - d + 1}K \oplus \tau _{\geq k + 1}K$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The vanishing of cohomology shows that we have a distinguished triangle 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \tau _{\leq k - d + 1}K \to K \to \tau _{\geq k + 1}K \to (\tau _{\leq k - d + 1}K)[1]  \]
</div>
    <p>
       By Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/05QT" data-tag="05QT">13.4.11</a> it suffices to show that the third arrow is zero. Thus it suffices to show that $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{D(R)}(\tau _{\geq k + 1}K, (\tau _{\leq k - d + 1}K)[1]) = 0$ which follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0FXJ" data-tag="0FXJ">15.81.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>